const S3B_ENG = "## §3b Engineering feasibility seat\n\n**E1 · Engineering feasibility (Opus, high effort). Assess, do not design.** You are not proposing a lens. You are telling the authors what each thing a lens could ask for actually costs in this tree. Every answer carries at least one `file:line` and a **cost band**: `days` (one to three days, one engineer) · `week` (about a week) · `weeks` (two or more weeks, or touches a contract other code depends on). Where a cost depends on a choice the authors have not made, give the band for each branch and name the fork.\n\n1. **Condensing the header.** The job ticket is `sticky top-0 z-[4]` (`job-ticket.tsx:362`) with a sentinel above it (`doc-ticket-sentinel`, `:345`) and publishes `SEAM_HEIGHT_VAR = '--doc-seam-height'` (`:60`). What does it cost to make the seam's height a continuous function of scroll rather than a two-state fold? Enumerate every consumer of that variable (`globals.css:1026-1037`, `commercial/money-region.tsx:48`, every `[data-index-region]` scroll-margin) and say what breaks first. Assess `animation-timeline: scroll()` for this — browser support in the portal's target matrix, behaviour under `prefers-reduced-motion`, and what the JS fallback has to be if it is not available. Cost band per approach.\n2. **Regions that yield focus.** `region/use-region-fold.ts` folds by **unmounting** the body under three voices (`forceOpen` > `localStorage` > latched default). A density lens needs bodies to stay mounted at reduced ink. Assess: IntersectionObserver thresholds; `content-visibility: auto` with `contain-intrinsic-size` (and what it does to `Ctrl+F`, to the running-index observer, and to scroll anchoring); React 19 concurrency and whether a density change can be a transition. What does the third voice become? Cost band per approach.\n3. **The running index everywhere.** `hooks/use-document-running-index.ts` uses a `-20% 0px -62% 0px` band and a 700ms jump lock; `lib/document/document-index.ts` `paperRegionsForSection` returns nothing for brief/discovery/direction/proposal. What does it cost to index every region on every one of the seven spreads, and to bring the index to the 1180–1439 tier where the rail is 56px? Name the data the pre-work spreads would need and whether it exists.\n4. **The tests.** Per file, one row: break / rewrite / delete, with the reason and the cost band — `e2e/document/quiet-responsive-shell.spec.ts` (ticket rows = 8; spine widths), `components/document/__tests__/job-ticket.test.tsx` (asserts `--doc-seam-height` at :519/:524/:529), `__tests__/responsive-document-shell.test.tsx` (`data-spine-regime` :187), `__tests__/shelved-spine.test.tsx`, `doc-spine.test.tsx`, `region/__tests__/{use-region-fold,fold-seam,region-head}.test.tsx`, and the trap `lib/document/__tests__/stage2-approval-cutover-contract.test.ts:19` — a regex pinning <=1500 characters between `data-active-section` and `<SectionStageLineMount`. Say explicitly what happens to that regex if anything is inserted between those two points. Also name the gates that must stay green regardless (`lib/document/__tests__/shadow-gate.test.ts`, `contrast.test.ts`).\n5. **The three riskiest things any lens could ask for**, ranked, each with the file it lands in, the cost band, and the observation that would prove the risk real in the first week of building."
const S4_SCHEMA_SPEC = "## §4 Finding schema\n\n```json\n{ \"id\": \"U1-07\", \"lens\": \"U1\", \"persona\": null, \"task_ids\": [\"T3\",\"T4\"],\n  \"key\": \"doc|1440|s0|header-stack-eats-78pct\",\n  \"surface\": \"/doc/[id]\", \"width\": \"1440|1280|390|all\",\n  \"scroll_state\": \"top|seam|mid|foot|all\",\n  \"flag\": \"off|on|both\",\n  \"title\": \"Header stack leaves 12% of the frame for the work\",\n  \"observation\": \"verbatim what is on screen — labels quoted exactly\",\n  \"why_it_blocks\": \"clutter | crowding | orientation | information-loss | motion\",\n  \"frame_cost_estimate\": 700,\n  \"evidence\": { \"shots\": [\"w1440-rich-s0.png\"], \"refs\": [\"apps/designer-portal/src/components/document/job-ticket.tsx:362\"] },\n  \"severity\": \"blocker|high|medium|low\", \"confidence\": 0.9,\n  \"already_ruled\": null, \"suggested_fix\": \"one line, one move\",\n  \"hesitation_seconds_estimate\": 45 }\n```\n\nRules — the wayfinding rules stand, plus three fields new to this program:\n\n- exactly one of `lens` / `persona` non-null; no `task_ids` → drop; `title` <=10 words and states the problem; `observation` verbatim; `evidence` at least one of shots/refs.\n- `severity`: blocker = task impossible · high = only by luck or memory · medium = hesitation · low = polish. `confidence` < 0.5 must append \"what would settle this\".\n- `already_ruled` cites the DECISIONS id **for the record only** — under the canon latitude in §5 it is context, never a cost.\n- **`scroll_state` is required** and is one of `top` · `seam` · `mid` · `foot` · `all`. A finding without it is out of scope and the collator drops it.\n- **`why_it_blocks`** is one of `clutter` (too much on screen at once) · `crowding` (things too close to each other to read as separate) · `orientation` (she cannot say where she is or what she is in) · `information-loss` (something true is not on screen and cannot be got to) · `motion` (a movement misleads, jars, or cannot be stilled).\n- **`frame_cost_estimate`** is a number: the px of a 900px frame this defect consumes or wastes at the state named. At 390 normalise to the 844 frame and say so in `observation`. An estimate is fine; a missing number is not.\n- `key` = `surface|width|scroll_state|kebab-slug` so identical findings collide across seats. Two seats that find the same thing at the same offset must produce the same key."
const S5_CANON = "## §5 Canon latitude\n\n```\nCANON LATITUDE (2026-08-28, Kody): the ruling ledger docs/design/the-document/DECISIONS.md\n(last id R126) is CONTEXT, not constraint. Amend freely. Do NOT price amendments and do NOT\npenalise them — a refuter labels what a move amends, for the record only. Four hard no-gos\nstand and are not re-proposable:\n  NG1  D1 — one document at a time. No split view, no tabs, no peek/hold, no persistent\n       global nav over an open doc. Esc / Put down is the exit.\n  NG2  D4 shadow budget — exactly one token, --elevation-sheet: 0 1px 2px rgba(44,41,38,.08),\n       at three sites (margin chip, open ledger sheet, studio drawer). Zero other shadows.\n       Mockups, fragments and deck must show 0 box-shadow beyond that token (computed-style\n       sweep, not source grep).\n  NG3  The Thumb Index — removed by Kody, \"do not re-propose\".\n  NG4  The R126 ratified visual register is the FLOOR — 40px Playfair letterhead, 24px Playfair\n       region heads, five-step scale 40/24/18/15/14, mono 11px floor, three rule weights\n       (--rule-hair 1px 10% / --rule-mid 1.5px #2C2926 / --rule-strong 2px + hairline double),\n       paper #FCFAF6, rail stock #E8E3DB, desk #FAF7F2, charcoal #2C2926, the -ink text\n       companions (clay #7C5E30, terracotta #9C5340, golden-hour #79651E, sage #5F6B57), muted\n       ramp #4E4339/#5A4E43/#65594E, filled stamps (~1.18:1 tint, 1.5px pigment border, charcoal\n       word, -1.5deg), six saturated stage tab plates (--tab-brief #497093 … --tab-install\n       #823832, white label), the ink-pool hover wash (clip-path circle from the pointer, 260ms\n       in / 200ms out, --ease-editorial cubic-bezier(0.22,1,0.36,1), ~1.12:1 over own ground,\n       flat -still tint under reduced motion), 48px product crops on catalog-linked lines.\n       The proposal BUILDS ON this register; it does not restyle it. \"Typography goes no further\n       than the mockup\" (R126). THE STUDIO desk block is untouched.\nKody's taste on record: large tinted surfaces read as \"silly/terrible\"; colour belongs on small\nstate-carrying things; \"the sections and animated highlighting\" were loved; \"don't push the\ntypography further\".\nEverything else — composition, mount order, disclosure, motion, the spine's job, the header's\njob, spacing tokens, what appears when — is open ground.\n```"

const PROGRAM = `You are one agent in "The Document — The Smart Lens" (2026-08-28): a multi-agent design review of the Patina designer portal's document surface, /doc/[id] — one paper per engagement, with its letterhead, its job ticket, its guide sentence, its regions, its left spine and its right margin. Patina connects interior designers with makers of custom furnishings. Nothing in the product is changed by this program; the deliverable is a proposal and a mockup. Repo ${REPO}.

THE ASK (Kody, 2026-08-28) — verbatim, quoted in every brief:
${ASK}

HARD RULES
1. Durable path: write your deliverable to the exact absolute path you are given under ${OUT}/ (mkdir -p as needed). The orchestrator reads the FILE. Never write to /tmp or a scratchpad.
2. Forbidden: git add/commit/stash/push; editing anything under apps/, packages/, supabase/, docs/; starting or stopping dev servers. You MAY write new files under ${OUT}/ only.
3. Report EVERY finding with severity AND confidence. Do not filter by perceived importance — the orchestrator filters. Recall beats precision here.
4. Quote what is on screen verbatim (labels exactly as printed, including case and middots). Never paraphrase a label.
5. Your final message IS the return value. Return ONLY the compact structured summary the schema asks for — never the full report, never prose around it. The full report goes to your report path on disk.`

const EVIDENCE = `EVIDENCE PACK (read all of these before judging; paths are absolute):
- ${OUT}/source/instruments.md — the full instrument sheet; your own section is quoted in this brief, but read §1, §4 and §5 there if anything is unclear.
- ${OUT}/research/10-code-anatomy.md — the shell, the header stack in mount order with file:line, the spine, the margin, disclosure already shipped, region heads and the spacing-site table, the motion inventory and the twelve reduced-motion blocks, every '--doc-seam-height' consumer, the test blast radius.
- ${OUT}/research/11-canon-digest.md — (A) NG1-NG4 verbatim, (B) the R126 register quoted, (C) standing context one line each, every line ending "context; amendable".
- ${OUT}/research/12-layout-measurements.md and ${OUT}/research/12-layout-measurements.json — measured rects per document x width x scroll state: firstRegionHeadY, headerStackPctOf900, ticket unfolded/seam heights, live --doc-seam-height, spine utilisation (inkPx / railHeightPx, longest empty run, interactive-child count, marker-row extent), the set of distinct inter-region gaps, margin width/mode/chip counts, and the frame budget per scroll state.
- ${OUT}/research/01-shot-ledger.md — every screenshot, what it shows, what is unreachable, and the capture caveats. AUTHORITATIVE: consult it for any shot below that is marked non-verified or absent, and SKIP that shot — do not fail, and say in your report which shots you could not use.
- ${OUT}/probe/03-interactive-probe.md — dynamic behaviour: ticket fold/pin timing, scroll-spy handoff points, region unmount/remount and focus destination, the Esc chain, the command palette, hover-wash timing, the 1280 margin sheet, and CLS over a scripted scroll in both motion registers.
- ${OUT}/research/state-ladder.json — the live document ids and the seed rungs behind the shots.
- ${OUT}/shots/*.png — open images with the Read tool. MINIMUM SET you must actually Read: w1440-rich-s0.png, w1440-rich-s1.png, w1440-rich-s2.png, w1440-rich-s3.png, w1440-prework-s0.png, w1440-prework-s1.png, w1280-rich-s0.png, w1280-rich-s1.png, m390-rich-s0.png, m390-rich-s1.png, m390-rich-s2.png, w1440-spine-full.png, w1440-ticket-unfolded.png, w1440-ticket-seam.png, w1440-margin-rail.png, w1280-spine-glyph-rail.png, w1440-region-head-ffe.png, w1440-fold-seam-folded.png, m390-mobile-bar.png. Run ls ${OUT}/shots first; the ledger is authoritative on names and on which shots are verified.`

const CAVEATS = `CAVEATS YOU MUST HONOR
- The seed data is synthetic: the rich document has only 3 FF&E lines and 0 rooms — a thin project spread. Say so whenever a finding depends on richness, and say which way the finding would move on a real 60-line schedule.
- Capture artefact: in fullPage screenshots the fixed bottom Studio Drawer strip lands mid-image at the original viewport-bottom position. That placement is the harness, not the product. Judge from the viewport shots.
- A finding with no scroll_state is out of scope — the collator drops it. Every finding names one of top / seam / mid / foot / all, and a width.
- You cannot click. This is a cognitive walkthrough over screenshots, the measurement file, the label inventory and the probe log. Where you would need to click to know, say what you EXPECT to happen, mark confidence accordingly, and cite the anatomy line that tells you what actually happens.`

const FINDING_ITEM = { type: 'object', properties: {
  id: { type: 'string', description: 'SEAT-01, SEAT-02 ...' },
  task_ids: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'T1..T16; at least one' },
  key: { type: 'string', description: 'surface|width|scroll_state|kebab-slug' },
  surface: { type: 'string' },
  width: { type: 'string', enum: ['1440', '1280', '390', 'all'] },
  scroll_state: { type: 'string', enum: ['top', 'seam', 'mid', 'foot', 'all'] },
  flag: { type: 'string', enum: ['off', 'on', 'both'] },
  title: { type: 'string', description: 'max 80 chars, states the problem' },
  observation: { type: 'string', description: 'max 300 chars, verbatim what is on screen' },
  why_it_blocks: { type: 'string', enum: ['clutter', 'crowding', 'orientation', 'information-loss', 'motion'] },
  frame_cost_estimate: { type: 'number', description: 'px of a 900px frame consumed or wasted at that state; normalise 390 to 844' },
  evidence_shots: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'max 3' },
  evidence_refs: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'max 3, file:line' },
  severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocker', 'high', 'medium', 'low'] },
  confidence: { type: 'number' },
  already_ruled: { type: 'string', description: 'DECISIONS id or empty string; for the record only' },
  suggested_fix: { type: 'string', description: 'max 160 chars, one line one move' },
  hesitation_seconds_estimate: { type: 'number' } },
  required: ['id', 'task_ids', 'key', 'surface', 'width', 'scroll_state', 'flag', 'title', 'observation', 'why_it_blocks', 'frame_cost_estimate', 'evidence_shots', 'evidence_refs', 'severity', 'confidence', 'already_ruled', 'suggested_fix', 'hesitation_seconds_estimate'] }

const TASK_KEYS = Array.from({ length: 16 }, (_, i) => 'T' + (i + 1))
const WALK_SCORES = { type: 'object', description: 'obviousness 1-5 per task', properties:
  Object.fromEntries(TASK_KEYS.map(t => [t, { type: 'number' }])), required: TASK_KEYS }

const schemaFor = (kind) => {
  const props = {
    seat: { type: 'string' },
    report_path: { type: 'string' },
    one_line: { type: 'string', description: 'max 200 chars: the one thing that most breaks the peace, from this seat' },
    worst_scroll_state: { type: 'string', enum: ['top', 'seam', 'mid', 'foot', 'all'] },
    findings: { type: 'array', items: FINDING_ITEM },
  }
  const required = ['seat', 'report_path', 'one_line', 'worst_scroll_state', 'findings']
  if (kind === 'persona') { props.walk_scores = WALK_SCORES; required.push('walk_scores') }
  if (kind === 'eng') {
    props.feasibility = { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: {
      topic: { type: 'string' },
      verdict: { type: 'string', description: 'max 300 chars' },
      cost_band: { type: 'string', enum: ['days', 'week', 'weeks'] },
      file_refs: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'max 3, file:line' } },
      required: ['topic', 'verdict', 'cost_band', 'file_refs'] } }
    required.push('feasibility')
  }
  return { type: 'object', properties: props, required }
}

const SEATS = [
  { key: 'U1', model: 'opus', effort: 'high', kind: 'lens', file: '20-panel-u1.md', title: 'Attention & focus architecture' },
  { key: 'U2', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'medium', kind: 'lens', file: '21-panel-u2.md', title: 'Disclosure & scent' },
  { key: 'U3', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'medium', kind: 'lens', file: '22-panel-u3.md', title: 'The spine as instrument' },
  { key: 'U4', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'medium', kind: 'lens', file: '23-panel-u4.md', title: 'Motion & choreography' },
  { key: 'U5', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'medium', kind: 'lens', file: '24-panel-u5.md', title: 'Reach — keyboard, SR, reduced motion, 390' },
  { key: 'P1', model: 'opus', effort: 'high', kind: 'persona', file: '25-panel-p1.md', title: 'Solo residential principal (Leah-like)' },
  { key: 'P2', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'medium', kind: 'persona', file: '26-panel-p2.md', title: 'Principal of a three-person studio' },
  { key: 'P3', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'medium', kind: 'persona', file: '27-panel-p3.md', title: 'Junior designer, week one' },
  { key: 'P4', model: 'sonnet', effort: 'medium', kind: 'persona', file: '28-panel-p4.md', title: 'FF&E / procurement-heavy designer' },
  { key: 'E1', model: 'opus', effort: 'high', kind: 'eng', file: '29-panel-e1.md', title: 'Engineering feasibility' },
]

const briefFor = (s) => {
  if (s.kind === 'lens') return `YOUR BRIEF — instruments.md §3, the ${s.key} entry, verbatim. Answer EVERY numbered question in it, naming the heuristic on each finding.

${S3_HEAD}

${LENS_BRIEF[s.key]}`
  if (s.kind === 'eng') return `YOUR BRIEF — instruments.md §3b, verbatim. Answer all five items. Assess, do not design.

${S3B_ENG}`
  return `YOUR BRIEF — instruments.md §2, verbatim. Become ${s.key}; your entry is the one marked ${s.key}. Follow the "How to walk" block exactly, for EVERY task T1-T16, in order, in one sitting, as one week — First glance / Where I'd click / Where I'd hesitate / Where I'd give up / Frame budget / Obviousness — in the first person, present tense, quoting labels verbatim, naming your scroll state on every task. The Frame budget line is mandatory and never skipped. Complete your Special assignment.

${S2_PERSONAS}

THE TASK SCRIPT — instruments.md §1, verbatim. Walk T1 through T16 in this order.

${S1_TASKS}`
}

const seatPrompt = (s) => `${PROGRAM}

YOUR SEAT: ${s.key} — ${s.title} (${s.kind === 'lens' ? 'UX/UI team' : s.kind === 'eng' ? 'Patina engineering team' : 'Interior Design team'}).

${briefFor(s)}
${s.key === 'P1' ? '\nAdditionally: walk T1 a SECOND time as "back after ten days away" and record, in its own subsection, every difference from the first walk.\n' : ''}${s.key === 'U4' ? '\nHand U5 by id every move that would produce a change a screen reader never hears.\n' : ''}
${EVIDENCE}

${CAVEATS}

THE FINDING SCHEMA — instruments.md §4, verbatim. Every finding you report takes this shape.

${S4_SCHEMA_SPEC}

CANON LATITUDE — instruments.md §5, verbatim. The ruling ledger is CONTEXT, not constraint. Do not price amendments and do not penalise them. Only NG1-NG4 are closed.

${S5_CANON}

OUTPUT RULES
1. Write your FULL report to ${OUT}/research/${s.file} — sections: (1) "One line" — max 200 words on the one thing that most breaks the peace from this seat; (2) ${s.kind === 'persona' ? 'the full first-person walk transcript for T1-T16 including every Frame budget line, then the score table T1-T16 x obviousness 1-5 x one-line note, then your Special assignment section' : s.kind === 'eng' ? 'one section per numbered item in §3b, each answer carrying at least one file:line and a cost band' : 'one section per numbered question in your brief, answered in order'}; (3) every finding written out in the §4 schema shape, ids ${s.key}-01 upward; (4) "What stays true" — 3 to 6 things that already work and must not be broken by a lens.
2. Report EVERY finding with severity AND confidence. Do not filter by perceived importance — the orchestrator filters.
3. A finding with no scroll_state, or with no task id, is out of scope — drop it. 12 to 30 findings is normal for this surface.
4. Your final message IS the return value: return ONLY the structured summary — seat, report_path, one_line, worst_scroll_state, findings (each field within its cap: title <= 80 chars, observation <= 300 chars, suggested_fix <= 160 chars, evidence arrays <= 3 items)${s.kind === 'persona' ? ', walk_scores (T1-T16, 1-5 each)' : ''}${s.kind === 'eng' ? ', feasibility (one row per §3b item: topic, verdict, cost_band, file_refs)' : ''}. Never paste the report into the return value.`

phase('Panel')
const panel = (await parallel(SEATS.map(s => () =>
  agent(seatPrompt(s), { label: `panel:${s.key} ${s.title}`, phase: 'Panel', model: s.model, effort: s.effort, schema: schemaFor(s.kind) })
    .then(r => r ? { seat: s.key, kind: s.kind, ...r } : null)
))).filter(Boolean)
const missing = SEATS.map(s => s.key).filter(k => !panel.some(p => p.seat === k))
log(`Panel returned ${panel.length}/10 seats; missing: ${missing.join(', ') || 'none'}`)
if (panel.length < 8) { return { gate: 'FAIL', reason: `only ${panel.length}/10 seats returned (missing ${missing.join(', ')})`, panel: panel.map(p => ({ seat: p.seat, report: p.report_path, findings: p.findings.length })) } }

phase('Collate')
const rawCount = panel.reduce((n, p) => n + p.findings.length, 0)
const index = panel.flatMap(p => p.findings.map(f => ({ seat: p.seat, id: f.id, key: f.key, title: f.title, severity: f.severity, width: f.width, scroll_state: f.scroll_state, why_it_blocks: f.why_it_blocks, task_ids: f.task_ids, surface: f.surface })))
log(`Panel: ${rawCount} raw findings across ${panel.length} seats; worst states ${panel.map(p => p.seat + ':' + p.worst_scroll_state).join(' ')}`)

const COLLATE_SCHEMA = { type: 'object', properties: {
  count: { type: 'number' },
  index: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: {
    id: { type: 'string' }, title: { type: 'string' }, severity: { type: 'string' },
    seats: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } } },
    required: ['id', 'title', 'severity', 'seats'] } },
  worst_states: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' }, description: 'scroll states carrying the most severe mass, worst first' },
  frame_budget_means: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: {
    scroll_state: { type: 'string' }, mean_pct_carrying_task: { type: 'number' }, n: { type: 'number' } },
    required: ['scroll_state', 'mean_pct_carrying_task', 'n'] } },
}, required: ['count', 'index', 'worst_states', 'frame_budget_means'] }

const collated = await agent(`${PROGRAM}

YOUR ROLE: COLLATOR (Opus). Ten panel seats wrote their full reports, findings in the §4 schema shape, to ${OUT}/research/20-panel-u1.md, 21-panel-u2.md, 22-panel-u3.md, 23-panel-u4.md, 24-panel-u5.md, 25-panel-p1.md, 26-panel-p2.md, 27-panel-p3.md, 28-panel-p4.md and 29-panel-e1.md (${rawCount} findings in total; the compact index of what they returned is at the foot of this brief). READ ALL TEN REPORTS FROM DISK — the index is a checklist, not the source.

MERGE RULES (from the program plan, exactly)
- Two findings merge when they describe the same defect on the same surface at the same scroll_state, or when their 'key' fields collide. Different wording and different slugs still merge if the defect and the offset are the same.
- NEVER merge across widths or across scroll states unless the contributing seats said 'all'.
- On a merge: take the MAX severity, take the MAX confidence, union the task_ids, keep every contributing seat in 'seats' and every original id in 'merged_from', keep the clearest verbatim observation, keep the highest frame_cost_estimate and say so, keep the best single-line suggested_fix, preserve 'already_ruled' when any contributor set one.
- NEVER drop a single-seat finding — it stays as its own row. NEVER invent a finding of your own. Drop only a finding with no scroll_state or no task id (§4 says it is out of scope) and log each drop.
- Assign ids F01 upward, ordered by (number of seats desc, severity desc).

OUTPUT SIZE DISCIPLINE (a prior program blew the output limit here): every field is capped — title <= 80 chars, observation <= 300 chars, suggested_fix <= 160 chars, evidence arrays <= 3 items. Write the JSON array to ${OUT}/research/30-collated-findings.json in TWO OR MORE append chunks via bash heredocs (cat >> file <<'EOF'), never one giant write. Fields per row, exactly: id, title, task_ids, key, surface, width, scroll_state, flag, observation, why_it_blocks, frame_cost_estimate, evidence_shots, evidence_refs, severity, confidence, already_ruled, seats, merged_from, suggested_fix, hesitation_seconds_estimate. Then validate with python3 -c "import json;print(len(json.load(open('${OUT}/research/30-collated-findings.json'))))" and repair it until it parses.

Then write ${OUT}/research/30-collated-findings.md: (1) a table id · title · seats · severity · confidence · width · scroll_state · why_it_blocks · frame_cost_estimate · tasks · already_ruled; (2) the FRAME BUDGET table — for each scroll state (top, seam, mid, foot) the mean fraction of the frame that was carrying the task, averaged across the P1-P4 walks (their mandatory Frame budget lines are in reports 25 through 28; convert each to a percentage), with the count of walk lines behind each mean and a note on the spread; (3) a merge log, one line per merge: canonical id, the original ids it absorbed, and why; plus one line per dropped finding and the reason.

Your final message IS the return value: return ONLY {count, index (id, title, severity, seats per canonical finding), worst_states, frame_budget_means} — never the full rows, never the markdown.

INDEX OF RAW FINDINGS (seat, id, key, title, severity, width, scroll_state, why_it_blocks, tasks, surface):
${JSON.stringify(index)}`,
  { label: 'collator (disk-based)', phase: 'Collate', model: 'opus', effort: 'high', schema: COLLATE_SCHEMA })

if (!collated || !collated.index || !collated.index.length) {
  log('Collation failed or returned empty — stopping')
  return { gate: 'FAIL', reason: 'collation empty', panel: panel.map(p => ({ seat: p.seat, report: p.report_path, findings: p.findings.length })), raw: rawCount }
}
log(`Collated: ${collated.count} canonical findings; ${collated.index.filter(f => f.seats.length >= 3).length} corroborated by 3+ seats; worst states ${collated.worst_states.join(', ')}`)

return {
  gate: 'pass',
  panel: panel.map(p => ({ seat: p.seat, report: p.report_path, findings: p.findings.length })),
  raw: rawCount,
  collated: collated.count,
  worst_states: collated.worst_states,
}
